{:edb/db-before-t 2177 :edb/db-after-t 2178 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535490 50 #inst "2026-10-09T01:06:11.305Z" 13194139535490 true] [17592186078500 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351715 13194139535490 true] [17592186078500 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351722 13194139535490 true] [17592186078501 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351729 13194139535490 true] [17592186078662 1100 "A thermometer is placed into a water bath and displays a reading of $50\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$. The thermometer is then transferred to a $10\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$ water bath, and its reading begins to decrease. After $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is $40\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$. To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the thermometer to register a reading of $30\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$?\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t > 0$ is the time, in minutes, since the thermometer was transferred into the $10\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$ water bath.*" 13194139535490 false] [17592186078662 1100 "A thermometer is placed into a water bath and displays a reading of ${50}^{∘}C.$ The thermometer is then transferred to a ${10}^{∘}C$ water bath, and its reading begins to decrease. After $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${40}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the thermometer to register a reading of ${30}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the thermometer was transferred into the ${10}^{∘}C$ water bath.*" 13194139535490 true] [17592186078662 1145 "Newton's law gives $T(t)=10+Ce^{-kt}$. From $T(0)=50$, $C=40$. Then $T(5)=40$ gives $e^{-5k}=30/40=3/4$, so $k=\\ln(4/3)/5$. To reach $30\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$, solve $30=10+40e^{-kt}$, hence $e^{-kt}=1/2$. Thus\n\n$$t=\\frac{5\\ln 2}{\\ln(4/3)}\\approx12.047\\text{ minutes}.$$\n\nThe total time since transfer is $12$ minutes to the nearest minute." 13194139535490 false] [17592186078662 1145 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={10}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-10).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-10| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-10>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-10 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-10 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-10 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =10+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${50}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=50,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50 & =10+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 50 & =10+K \\\\ K & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=10+40{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${40}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=40,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40 & =10+40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 30 & =40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & ={e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & =-5k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the thermometer reads ${30}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =30 \\\\ 10+40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =30 \\\\ 40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =20 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the thermometer will read ${30}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}≈12\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute." 13194139535490 true] [17592186078663 1017 #edb/ref 17592186075287 13194139535490 false] [17592186078663 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351736 13194139535490 true] [17592186078663 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535490 true] [17592186078663 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535490 false] [17592186078663 1100 "The temperature $T(t)$, in degrees Celsius, of a cup of coffee can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dT}{dt} =-0.05(T - 25)$\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the coffee is $100\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$, calculate the temperature of the coffee after $6$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139535490 false] [17592186078663 1100 "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a cup of coffee can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.05(T-25)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the coffee is ${100}^{∘}C,$ calculate the temperature of the coffee after $6$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139535490 true] [17592186078663 1145 "Separating variables gives $\\ln|T-25|=-0.05t+C$, so $T(t)=25+Ae^{-0.05t}$. The initial condition $T(0)=100$ gives $A=75$. Therefore\n\n$$T(6)=25+75e^{-0.3}\\approx80.56\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C},$$\n\nwhich rounds to $81\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$." 13194139535490 false] [17592186078663 1145 "We're told that the temperature of the cup of coffee is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.05(T-25).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-0.05\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-\\int 0.05\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-25| & =-0.05t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-25>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-25 & ={e}^{-0.05t+C} \\\\ T-25 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.05t} \\\\ T-25 & =K{e}^{-0.05t} \\\\ T & =25+K{e}^{-0.05t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the coffee was ${100}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=100,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =25+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =25+K \\\\ K & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=25+75{e}^{-0.05t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the coffee after $t=6$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(6)=25+75{e}^{-0.05\\cdot 6}≈{81}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139535490 true] [17592186351715 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351716 13194139535490 true] [17592186351715 1089 "q-106936" 13194139535490 true] [17592186351715 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535490 true] [17592186351715 1100 "The rate at which a pot of hot oil cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the liquid in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${30}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the oil? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139535490 true] [17592186351715 1113 #uuid "660e9efe-0c2c-57ea-8c8f-8be8f8b69eca" 13194139535490 true] [17592186351715 1145 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the tem