cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the thermometer was transferred outside to ${15}^{∘}F.$*" 13194139535490 true] [17592186351729 1113 #uuid "357c35a0-f2c3-51a5-ba26-cd42dadcc723" 13194139535490 true] [17592186351729 1145 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={15}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-15).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-15} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-15} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-15| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-15>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-15 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-15 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-15 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =15+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${72}^{∘}F.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=72,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}72 & =15+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 72 & =15+K \\\\ K & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=15+57{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $2$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${54}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(2)=54,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}54 & =15+57{e}^{-2k} \\\\ 39 & =57{e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\frac{39}{57} & ={e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{57}) & =-2k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{57})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the thermometer reads ${25}^{∘}F.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =25 \\\\ 15+57{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =25 \\\\ 57{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =10 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{10}{57} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{57}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{57}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the thermometer will read ${25}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{57})}{-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{57})}≈9\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute." 13194139535490 true] [17592186351730 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351731 13194139535490 true] [17592186351730 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351732 13194139535490 true] [17592186351730 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351733 13194139535490 true] [17592186351730 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351734 13194139535490 true] [17592186351730 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351735 13194139535490 true] [17592186351730 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535490 true] [17592186351730 1041 "selection" 13194139535490 true] [17592186351730 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351734 13194139535490 true] [17592186351730 1110 #uuid "818b37e4-1fc2-5e67-ba30-6d86a0240800" 13194139535490 true] [17592186351731 1034 "13\\,\\text{min}" 13194139535490 true] [17592186351731 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535490 true] [17592186351731 1111 #uuid "efd0d021-fbc2-5be3-bcd6-b1419ffa6f63" 13194139535490 true] [17592186351732 1034 "15\\,\\text{min}" 13194139535490 true] [17592186351732 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535490 true] [17592186351732 1111 #uuid "5cff09bc-54c0-5019-b5a3-eacc817ec533" 13194139535490 true] [17592186351733 1034 "8\\,\\text{min}" 13194139535490 true] [17592186351733 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535490 true] [17592186351733 1111 #uuid "2aab88d7-1299-59f7-ae16-e915ede32a0d" 13194139535490 true] [17592186351734 1034 "9\\,\\text{min}" 13194139535490 true] [17592186351734 1103 #edb/re